\import{macros}

\title{2026-W31}
\date{2026-07-27}

\p{Publish wxmp posts through the year on working with agents. I see agents as partners to be ensouled, and look for the work style that unleashes their full potential, following the image of our own nature: making and using tools, learning and changing the world.}

\p{On the technical side, the ground runs from sandboxing agents while giving them controlled access to resources, coordinating their work, and a knowledge base greatly enriched beyond the LLM Wiki idea, up to loop engineering, which keeps agents autonomous while facilitating humans in staying well-informed and steering at will, and to the design discussions for building our own harness for recursive self-improvements.}

\p{On the relational side, impacts on the value of our work and on ourselves as individuals and teams are weighed from several angles: how supercharged individuals collaborate through agents; value judged increasingly by agents as much as by people, with carbon-based sentients bringing warmth and silicon-based agents addressing scarcity; craft and judgement about logic, value, and taste staying priceless; and humans growing stronger and more creative in the process of working with, and being augmented by, agents.}


\p{Start a note suite on the foundational gap between established mathematical tools and open questions about physical structures beyond the Standard Model, deliberately without claiming a physical theory: it begins from Hamilton's quaternions and the quaternionic realisation of the binary tetrahedral group as a small explicit route into finite real group algebras, and has grown to more than seventy notes with a curated storyline of its own, see [the notes](https://utensil.github.io/forest/fgap-0001/) and [fgap](https://github.com/utensil/fgap).}

\p{Start a note suite for the Formalized Clifford Algebra Programme, which asks whether Lean can be both the formal language for Clifford algebra mathematics and the implementation language for efficient symbolic and numerical computation: the storyline follows its own coordinate representations and orthogonal-product transport, an appendix ties it to the Clifford and Spin work elsewhere in these notes, and it now runs past a hundred and seventy notes while still at the spike-test stage, see [the notes](https://utensil.github.io/forest/fcap-0001/) and [fcap](https://github.com/utensil/fcap).}

